{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"In a whole-fund conversion, the mutual fund is reorganized into an ETF; this is different from a mutual fund simply adding an ETF share class. The fund’s notice and prospectus/supplement should spell out the process and dates—there is no universal timetable.\n\nAn investor holding shares directly with the fund may need to:\n\n- **Review notices promptly** for the conversion date, any election or response deadline, account restrictions, and what happens if no action is taken. Confirm the fund has current contact and tax-reporting details.\n- **Open or designate a brokerage account** if required. ETF shares generally trade through a broker, unlike directly held mutual-fund shares. Ask the fund or its transfer agent whether it can arrange a brokerage transfer, what information/forms are needed, and whether the investor must initiate it.\n- **Check trading access and timing.** Trading may be unavailable during processing; after conversion, buying or selling generally requires a brokerage account and occurs at market prices during exchange hours, potentially at a premium or discount to NAV.\n- **Ask how fractional shares are handled.** ETFs generally trade in whole shares on exchanges. The plan may cash out a fractional remainder or handle it another way; don’t assume it will transfer intact.\n\nFollow the fund’s own instructions and confirm any account, tax, or transfer implications with the fund/transfer agent and broker."}}
